Refit artist-lockers with static as the spine — and correct three claims the first pass got wrong
The artist-lockers fitting is revised to a second pass. Same commit (`3f68225`), same project,
nothing changed upstream. What changed is the organising question: **what has to be running when
somebody opens the page.**

## Why re-frame it

The first pass treated *static* as one property among several and ranked recommendations by pain.
Ranking by pain was right and is kept. But reading the repository as a static artifact turns out to
be load-bearing rather than descriptive — and sorting it that way immediately surfaced three
first-pass claims that do not survive contact with the code.

The new spine is the project's own `_config.yml` `exclude:` block, which names exhaustively what the
published thing does not contain: `bot`, `bin`, `worker`, every manifest, every lockfile. They drew
the artifact/apparatus line themselves, in configuration, more sharply than we drew it for them.
Everything now sorts into **apparatus** (ran before publication), **the artifact** (the bytes), and
**read-time dependencies** (three hostnames — the only place a third party can take something away).

## The three corrections

1. **The Cloudflare Worker is not in the runtime path.** The first pass said it "authenticates site
   visitors." It doesn't. `js/discord-auth.js` is a public-client PKCE flow that POSTs
   `discord.com/api/oauth2/token` straight from the browser. The worker is unreferenced, its
   `REDIRECT_URI` is still the `yourdomain.com` placeholder, and its CORS origin is hardcoded to the
   upstream author's Pages domain — wrong in every copy of a *template* repo. This is a correction in
   their favour: they already removed the server. It also drops the toolchain floor from four to
   three for free, which is now the cheapest item in the document.

2. **`acquireBlob()` is already the seam we told them to build.** The first pass's headline now-move
   was "one function answering where the bytes live, with Pinata behind it" — costed at an afternoon.
   That function exists in `js/player.js` with three tiers: OPFS, then the gateway, then the expiring
   CDN, cheapest and most durable first. The real remaining gap is one line: the gateway hostname is
   Liquid-templated in at build time, so *which gateway* is a rebuild rather than a value.

3. **The auth gate is cosmetic by construction and cannot be otherwise.** `index.html` emits every
   ref unconditionally into `data-player` attributes; the gate is an overlay `unlocker.js` hides.
   The bytes ship before the gate is drawn. So this stops being a preference we hoped they'd keep
   ("is auth a convenience or a prerequisite?") and becomes structural: a static host cannot gate, it
   can only decorate. The risk isn't them choosing wrong, it's them believing it gates.

## Other things the static reading turned up

- **Their README's first philosophy line is the one they're currently breaking.** *No third-party
  vendor support loaded by the frontend runtime* — and `js/player.js:64` fetches a Pinata gateway in
  the frontend runtime. Framed as the wedge it is, not a gotcha: every other vendor was designed out,
  this one survived because nothing else was on offer. It's the strongest bottles argument on the
  page and they wrote it themselves.
- **The OPFS cache is unexportable**, which is a better bottles argument than the first pass made.
  *Accumulate offline media caches* is realised in origin-private storage: genuinely durable, and
  impossible to hand to anyone or back up. The most durable artifact the project produces exists only
  where it can never leave. A bottle is the exportable form of exactly that.
  It also bounds any withdrawal promise they could make to artists — noted in the `voices` section.
- **Atlas should come off the table.** `index.html` carries `noindex, nofollow`. They've already
  voted on discoverability and we missed it.
- **The `_plugins` obstacle was overstated.** First pass said "they have `_plugins`" and stopped.
  Measured: 30 lines, two files, pure Liquid string filters, no Jekyll internals. Still not a
  recommendation to migrate to `jekyll-enough` — but the number is the finding, per this document's
  own standard.

## Findings added

The original six are kept verbatim. Three new ones, and the first is about us:

7. **A fitting written from the README describes a project that does not exist.** All three
   corrections came from code the README summarises accurately but incompletely. The first pass
   recommended two things they'd already done and credited them with one they hadn't. Every claim in
   this pass now names a file and, where it matters, a line.
8. **We assumed a server, because our vocabulary has one.** Engines, seats, relays, mailboxes all
   presume something that can be running. This project's answer to nearly every question is *it's a
   file that was already built*, and the first pass kept reaching past that.
9. **Their `exclude:` list states our own principle better than we do.** A project can hold a
   principle in a form we don't recognise as a statement of it. You only find that by reading the
   configuration, not the prose.

## What was verified, and how

Read at `3f68225` in a clean checkout: `_config.yml`, `index.html`, `_includes/{auth,locker,slim_record}.html`,
`js/{player,unlocker,discord-auth}.js`, `worker/{discord-auth.js,wrangler.toml}`, `bot/cdn.py`,
`bin/deploy-discord-auth`, `_plugins/*.rb`, `Gemfile`, and a sample `refs.json`.
The "worker is unreferenced" claim was checked by grepping `js/`, `_includes/`, `index.html` and both
config files for any worker or `workers.dev` URL — only the `_config.yml` exclude entry matches.
All cross-document links (`../trade.md`, `../../BOTTLES.md` §1.2/§2/§3, `../../OPEN.md` §10,
`../../ADOPTING.md`) were checked to still resolve to existing anchors.

## Deliberately not done

- **Nothing was changed in artist-lockers itself**, and nothing here was asked of them. This is still
  a fitting published whole, and it still holds `status: draft`.
- **No petition filed** for the "one project, three categories' worth of obligation" problem — it's
  already `OPEN.md` §10 and this pass only added the OPFS-withdrawal wrinkle to it.
- The dead `worker/` is described as worth deleting, but that's their repository and their call; it
  is written as a ledger item, not a patch.
